{
	title: 'Open or close blocks: kinds, majority rule, live hint, Tab',
	spec: `The /toggle dialog and syntax, on the terminal and the web alike. The user-facing term is "block"; the dialog title is "Open or close blocks".

Syntax: an optional '#', optional kind letters, then a number, a range N-M (inclusive) or '*'. Kinds: t tool, r reasoning (thinking), a assistant, u user, m message from another session, s system, q question. Letters combine: au10-40 means the user and assistant blocks in 10-40; t* means every tool block. A single number names one block of any kind; a letter before it is ignored (#t24, t24, 24 are the same). A range or '*' without letters never touches user or assistant blocks; naming a or u includes them. An empty target is the latest tool block.

Majority rule: a range or '*' opens every matched block when more than half are closed, otherwise closes them all; a tie opens them, since opening loses nothing. One named block flips. Fold state stays per client, so each client applies the rule to its own state.

Dialog: the field; below it a live hint that updates on every keystroke and says what Enter does ("Opens 12 tool blocks: t4-t88") or why the input is invalid; then a help area:
  24, #t24   one block          t*   all tool blocks
  10-24      a range            r*   all thinking (reasoning) blocks
  empty      latest tool block  Tab  complete a block id
  User and assistant blocks change only when you name them (a, u).
  Kinds: tool, reasoning, assistant, user, message, system, question
The kinds row separates words with commas, like the terminal's key help row; each word's first letter (the kind letter) is bright and bold. The placeholder is static ("24, 10-24 or t*").

Tab completes like /command completion: it fills the common prefix of the matching block ids; when several match it lists them in the help area, packed several per row, newest first, at least 10; more than fit end with "+N more". It never cycles.

Size: the terminal dialog is as tall as its content (field, hint, help or completions); the web dialog is as tall as its content too, not the full picker height.

The model runs /toggle through the command tool with the same syntax. The host resolves the target against history and returns the matched block ids ("12 tool blocks: t4, t7, ..."), not opened or closed, since fold state is per client; it then tells the following clients to apply the target.`,
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['ghs', 'r4d'],
	foldInto: ['ghs']
}
